{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0f26f0bc68fceba10169bb42dc18e08191a5067f29aa440c9e", "created": 1773880028, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Early-Stage Cervical Cancer (Stage 1A1–1B2, 2A1): Surgical Pathways and Nodal Evaluation\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community is a clinical-treatment knowledge cluster focused on early-stage cervical cancer substages (Stage 1A1, 1A2, 1B1, 1B2 and Stage 2A1) and the surgical procedures used to diagnose, stage, and treat them. Stage definitions connect into broader stage groupings (Stage 1 and Stage 2A), while decision-driving risk features—particularly lymphovascular space invasion (LVSI) and cone biopsy margin status—route patients toward more conservative options (cone biopsy, extrafascial/simple hysterectomy) or more extensive operations (modified radical hysterectomy, radical hysterectomy, radical trachelectomy). Pelvic lymph node evaluation appears as a recurring companion procedure across multiple pathways, reflecting its central role in assessing spread and guiding the overall surgical plan. [Data: Entities (4847, 4848, 4849, 4850, 4861, +more); Relationships (10648, 10649, 10650, 10651, 10654, +more)]\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Stage substaging structures the community and anchors treatment pathways\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The network is organized around cervical cancer substages that explicitly map into broader stage categories, creating a backbone for subsequent management decisions. Stage 1A1, 1A2, 1B1, and 1B2 are each represented as substages within Stage 1, while Stage 2A1 is represented within Stage 2A. [Data: Entities (4847, 4848, 4849, 4850, 4861); Relationships (10648, 10649, 10650, 10651, 10654)]\\n\\nThis staging backbone is not merely classificatory; it is repeatedly referenced as the context in which specific procedures (cone biopsy, hysterectomy variants, radical trachelectomy) become appropriate or inappropriate depending on risk features and clinical aims (e.g., fertility preservation). [Data: Entities (4733, 4755, 4753, 4756, 4757); Relationships (10617, 10623, 10624, 10563, 10559)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Cone biopsy functions as both a diagnostic and, in select cases, therapeutic procedure\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Cone biopsy is described as a procedure that removes a cone-shaped section of the cervix to clarify extent of disease and assess whether cancer is present at tissue margins. This diagnostic role is central for very early disease (especially stage 1A1) and can also serve as definitive treatment in some early/small cancers when risk is low. [Data: Entities (4733, 4847, 4854); Relationships (10608)]\\n\\nThe community links cone biopsy to subsequent decision points through margin assessment: when margins are not adequate or staging remains uncertain, repeat cone biopsy may be pursued, while margin status can also drive escalation to hysterectomy or brachytherapy if surgery is not possible. [Data: Entities (4854, 4847, 4733); Relationships (10608)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Margin status is an explicit pivot that drives escalation or de-escalation after cone biopsy\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Margins—defined as the edges of removed tissue—are specifically highlighted as a determinant of whether observation is possible, whether additional cone biopsy is recommended, or whether hysterectomy/brachytherapy becomes advisable when surgery is not possible. This makes margins an operational decision variable within the cluster rather than a passive pathology descriptor. [Data: Entities (4854, 4733); Relationships (10608)]\\n\\nThis margin-driven branching contributes to the overall logic of the community: initial conservative management (cone biopsy) can be sufficient when margins are clear and other risk features are favorable, while non-clear margins or other adverse findings can justify broader surgical interventions. [Data: Entities (4854, 4756, 4733); Relationships (10608, 10623)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"LVSI is a key risk feature that shifts management toward more extensive surgery\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Lymphovascular space invasion (LVSI) is explicitly defined as a risk feature whose presence affects eligibility for less extensive approaches and increases recommendations toward more extensive surgery. This is concretely operationalized for stage 1A1: when LVSI is present, modified radical hysterectomy (with lymph node evaluation) is recommended. [Data: Entities (4855, 4847, 4756); Relationships (10611, 10623)]\\n\\nLVSI is also embedded in the descriptions of stage 1A1 and 1A2 management as a factor that makes treatment more aggressive (e.g., repeat cone biopsy or radical trachelectomy plus pelvic lymph node evaluation) compared with LVSI-negative scenarios that may allow observation after cone biopsy or less extensive surgery. [Data: Entities (4847, 4848, 4753, 4845, 4855); Relationships (10563, 10559, 10611)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Pelvic lymph node evaluation is the most recurrent “companion” procedure across early-stage pathways\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Pelvic lymph node evaluation is repeatedly presented as being performed alongside multiple primary procedures, including cone biopsy (in several stage 1A2 and 1B1 scenarios) and radical trachelectomy (when recommended/planned). This positions nodal evaluation as a cross-cutting staging and risk-assessment element within the community. [Data: Entities (4845, 4733, 4753); Relationships (10563, 10559, 10538)]\\n\\nThe relationships also clarify that pelvic lymph node evaluation is performed to assess (and sometimes remove) pelvic lymph nodes, highlighting its dual diagnostic/therapeutic function depending on findings and surgical context. [Data: Relationships (10538)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Extrafascial (simple) hysterectomy is the common definitive option for stage IA1 when fertility is not desired, and is selectively used in other low-risk cases\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Extrafascial (simple) hysterectomy is described as less extensive than radical procedures (removing uterus and cervix without the wider tissue removal) and is most commonly used for very early cervical cancer, particularly stage IA1 when fertility preservation is not desired. [Data: Entities (4755, 4745); Relationships (10617, 10301)]\\n\\nIt is also described as a possible option in carefully selected low-risk situations for stage 1A2 or 1B1 when strict criteria are met, and it may be performed with pelvic lymph node evaluation as part of staging and risk assessment. [Data: Entities (4755, 4848, 4849, 4845); Relationships (10563, 10617)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Modified radical hysterectomy is a targeted escalation for stage 1A1 with LVSI and involves limited parametrial/vaginal resection\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Modified radical hysterectomy is defined as removing the uterus (including cervix) plus a limited portion of surrounding supportive connective tissue (parametrial tissue) and a small segment of upper vagina, generally via an abdominal approach. It is explicitly recommended for stage 1A1 cancers with LVSI, typically with lymph node evaluation. [Data: Entities (4756, 4847, 4855, 4754); Relationships (10623, 10611, 10304)]\\n\\nThe community also includes the anatomical rationale: modified radical hysterectomy removes a portion of the connective tissue supporting the cervix, linking procedure choice to the extent of local tissue involvement/risk management. [Data: Entities (4754, 4756); Relationships (10304)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Radical hysterectomy is positioned as the definitive surgical standard as risk/stage increases (including 1B2 and select 2A1)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Radical hysterectomy is described as an extensive hysterectomy removing the uterus/cervix plus much of the surrounding connective tissue (parametrial tissue) and the upper portion of the vagina, usually with pelvic lymph node evaluation and sometimes para-aortic node removal. It is recommended for certain early-stage diseases including stage 1B2 and 2A1, and for stage 1A2 cancers that do not meet low-risk criteria. [Data: Entities (4757, 4850, 4861, 4848); Relationships (10624)]\\n\\nStage 1B2 is specifically characterized as typically managed with definitive surgery rather than fertility-sparing approaches, with radical hysterectomy plus pelvic lymph node evaluation as standard and possible para-aortic lymph node removal depending on risk factors. [Data: Entities (4850, 4757, 4845)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 3.2,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"Impact is relatively low because the community is a clinical decision/treatment knowledge cluster (not an operational or adversarial network), though it is important due to direct implications for surgical management pathways and patient outcomes. [Data: Entities (4757, 4755, 4756, 4753, 4845, +more); Relationships (10617, 10623, 10624, 10563, 10559, +more)]\"\n}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 2055, "prompt_tokens": 4867, "total_tokens": 6922, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 1536, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}